+# Fix Windows Local Dev — Spec
+
+**Date**: 2026-07-16
+**Status**: Draft
+**Ticket**: EPMCDME-13549
+**Slug**: fix-windows-local-dev
+**Complexity**: M (17/36)
+
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Codebase findings from technical analysis:
+
+- `docker-compose.yml` uses `${HOME}` in the GCP credentials secret path (line 187) and defaults `INSTALL_ENTERPRISE=true`. Docker Compose validates secrets globally at parse time — this blocks even `docker compose up postgres elasticsearch` on Windows where `HOME` is not set as an OS environment variable. The fix requires no change to `docker-compose.yml`: Windows developers add `HOME=C:\Users\<username>` (their `USERPROFILE` value) to `.env`. Docker Compose reads `.env` before the shell environment, so `${HOME}` resolves correctly. On Linux/macOS `HOME` is already set in the shell and takes precedence over `.env`, so no action is needed on those platforms.
+- `README.md` instructs `cd src/` before running uvicorn (line 97). Poetry's `packages` config makes this unnecessary; running from `src/` silently breaks JWT key and storage dir resolution.
+- `AGENTS.md` Shell rule (line 77) tells all agents to use bash/Linux syntax. The development host is Windows 11. Every AI-generated command fails in PowerShell without this being corrected.
+- No step-by-step `setup-guide.md` exists; no `make migrate` Makefile target; no `.gitattributes` to prevent CRLF corruption of shell scripts on Windows clone.
+- `CALLBACK_API_BASE_URL` defaults to `http://host.docker.internal:8080` — correct only for Workflow 2 (backend inside Docker); incorrect for Workflow 1 and must be documented.
+- Two local-dev workflows are already implicit in the project (`docker-compose.yml`, `Makefile`) but never made explicit in documentation.
+- Application source code in `src/` requires no changes.
+- `poetry sync` (available since Poetry 1.7) is preferred over `poetry install` for local dev: it installs from the lockfile and removes packages not in it, producing a clean reproducible environment.
+- `.env.example` and `.env.local` are out of scope (separate ticket). The committed `.env` is the single reference and override file for this ticket.
+
+---
+
+## Story
+
+**As a** Windows developer onboarding to the CodeMie backend,  
+**I want** clear, accurate, Windows-compatible setup instructions and a working `docker-compose.yml`,  
+**so that** I can build and run the backend successfully using either the local-backend or fully-dockerized workflow without trial-and-error or platform-specific workarounds.
+
+---
+
+## Background
+
+Windows developers cannot follow the existing README to get the backend running: `docker compose up postgres elasticsearch` fails at parse time due to `${HOME}` in the secrets block, and even if bypassed, the incorrect `cd src/` uvicorn instruction silently breaks JWT auth. There are no Windows-specific notes, no step-by-step setup guide, and AI agent assistance is actively harmful (AGENTS.md instructs bash syntax on a PowerShell host). Two use cases — local backend dev vs. fully-dockerized backend — are never distinguished, causing developers to configure env vars incorrectly for their workflow.
+
+---
+
+## Acceptance Criteria
+
+### Workflow 1 — Local backend + containers
+
+- [ ] Given a fresh Windows clone where the developer has added `HOME=C:\Users\<username>` to `.env`, when a developer runs `docker compose up postgres elasticsearch`, then both containers start without error (Docker Compose resolves `${HOME}` via `.env`; no change to `docker-compose.yml` required).
+- [ ] Given Workflow 1, when the developer runs the backend from the repo root using `make run` or `poetry run uvicorn codemie.rest_api.main:app ...`, then the backend starts, stays running, and JWT auth resolves `.keys/jwt_*.pem` correctly (no `cd src/` instruction in the setup path).
+- [ ] Given Workflow 1, when the developer sets up the Python environment, then the documented command is `poetry sync` (not `poetry install`) so that the lockfile-exact set of packages is installed and no stale packages remain.
+- [ ] Given Workflow 1, when the developer has not previously run database migrations, then README clearly lists `make migrate` (or the cross-platform alembic command) as a required step before starting the backend.
+- [ ] Given Workflow 1, when the developer reviews `.env` configuration guidance, then README or `setup-guide.md` clearly documents that `CALLBACK_API_BASE_URL` must be overridden to `http://localhost:8080` in `.env` (the committed default `host.docker.internal` is correct only for Workflow 2).
+
+### Workflow 2 — Fully dockerized backend
+
+- [ ] Given a fresh Windows clone where the developer has `HOME=C:\Users\<username>` set in `.env` (same prerequisite as Workflow 1), when a developer runs `docker compose up --build codemie postgres elasticsearch`, then the build and startup complete without error and the backend container stays running (`INSTALL_ENTERPRISE` defaults to `false` so no enterprise packages are fetched by default).
+- [ ] Given Workflow 2 with the enterprise extra needed, then README provides an explicit opt-in section documenting the additional steps: setting `INSTALL_ENTERPRISE=true` as a build arg. The GCP credentials file is already set up as part of standard dev prerequisites, so no additional credential setup is needed for enterprise opt-in beyond enabling the flag.
+
+### Documentation quality
+
+- [ ] Given README.md, when a developer reads it, then there are clearly separated sections for Workflow 1 and Workflow 2, each with explicit step-by-step prerequisites and commands.
+- [ ] Given README.md and `setup-guide.md`, when they are reviewed together, then there are no contradictions in commands, env var names, or workflow guidance between the two documents.
+- [ ] Given README.md, when shell commands are listed, then each bash-only command that does not work in PowerShell is accompanied by a Windows-compatible alternative (e.g., `$env:VAR = "value"` instead of `export VAR=value`; `Remove-Item -Recurse` instead of `rm -rf`).
+- [ ] Given a fresh Windows clone using the Git for Windows default `core.autocrlf=true`, when `scripts/git-hooks/pre_commit.sh` is executed, then it runs without a "bad interpreter: No such file or directory" error (`.gitattributes` enforces LF for `*.sh`).
+
+### Agent guidance
+
+- [ ] Given `AGENTS.md` Shell rule, when an AI agent generates a local development command, then the rule instructs both PowerShell-compatible syntax for Windows hosts and bash-compatible syntax for Linux/macOS hosts.
+- [ ] Given `.ai-run/guides/development/setup-guide.md`, when an AI agent needs to bootstrap the local dev environment, then the guide provides a complete, ordered, cross-platform step sequence for both Workflow 1 and Workflow 2.
+
+### Regression
+
+- [ ] Given the existing CI-equivalent quality gates (`make lint`, `make test`, or equivalent), when the changes are applied, then no gate that was previously passing becomes failing.
+
+---
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Fixing or making unit or integration tests pass on Windows (separate ticket).
+- Adding automated CI/CD pipeline or Windows-specific test workflows.
+- Fixing `pytest.ini` default `PG_URL` port placeholder (separate cleanup).
+- Changes to `src/` application code.
+- LiteLLM, Jaeger, Kibana, Prometheus, Grafana, or Pyroscope service setup (not part of the minimal local-dev stack).
+- Creating `.env.example` or `.env.local` (separate ticket). The committed `.env` is the single reference and override file.
+- Fixing GNU make installation docs beyond a brief prerequisite note.
+
+---
+
+## Implementation Notes
+
+These are codebase facts from the technical analysis, not design decisions:
+
+- **`docker-compose.yml` GCP credentials — no change required**: `${HOME}` stays as-is. The fix is documentation only: add a commented `HOME=` placeholder to the committed `.env` (e.g. `# Windows: HOME=C:\Users\<your-username>`) so Windows developers know to set it. On Windows, `USERPROFILE` holds the user home path and gcloud stores ADC at `%USERPROFILE%\.config\gcloud\application_default_credentials.json` — the same `.config\gcloud\` subpath as Linux. Setting `HOME` to the `USERPROFILE` value makes `${HOME}/.config/gcloud/...` resolve to the correct file on Windows. A stub `{}` JSON file works as a placeholder for developers without real GCP access.
+- **`docker-compose.yml` enterprise default**: Change `INSTALL_ENTERPRISE` build arg default from `true` to `false`. Enterprise opt-in is documented in a separate section: pass `--build-arg INSTALL_ENTERPRISE=true` when needed. GCP credentials are already in place (standard prerequisite), so enterprise opt-in requires only enabling the flag.
+- **`poetry sync`**: All documentation and `setup-guide.md` steps must use `poetry sync` for environment setup. `poetry install` should not appear in the recommended developer workflow.
+- **`Makefile` migrate target**: Alembic must be run as `poetry run alembic -c src/external/alembic/alembic.ini upgrade head` from the repo root. A `make migrate` target wrapping this command eliminates the OS-specific directory-change workaround.
+- **Run from repo root**: All `poetry run` and `make run` invocations must remain at repo root. `.env`, `.keys/`, `codemie-storage/`, and `codemie-repos/` are all resolved relative to CWD.
+- **`.env` as single config file**: The committed `.env` is both the default and the personal override file for this ticket. Workflow-specific values that differ from defaults (e.g. `CALLBACK_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080` for Workflow 1, `HOME=C:\Users\<username>` for Windows) are documented in `setup-guide.md` with clear instructions to edit `.env`. No `.env.local` or `.env.example` is introduced in this ticket.
+
+---
+
+## Open Questions
+
+- Should `setup-guide.md` replace or supplement the relevant README sections? (Recommend: `setup-guide.md` becomes the canonical step-by-step reference; README provides a brief Quick Start with a pointer to it.)
+- Should the enterprise opt-in be a separate compose override file (`docker-compose.enterprise.yml`) or a documented `--build-arg` flag on the base compose file?
